<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE Configure PUBLIC "-//Jetty//Configure//EN" "https://jetty.org/configure_10_0.dtd">

<!--
    The DataSource, which in a real deployment is the container's and is why
    xlet asks for one by JNDI name rather than taking a URL: the pool, the
    credentials and the lifecycle belong to whoever runs the application.

    This is the Jetty spelling of it. Tomcat says the same thing as a <Resource>
    in context.xml, WildFly as a datasource in its configuration, and Spring Boot
    as a bean - the servlet does not know or care which, it looks up
    java:comp/env/jdbc/xldr and finds whatever the container bound there.

    An in-memory H2, created on first connection and gone when the JVM stops,
    with the table the sample spec loads into. DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1 is what keeps it
    alive between connections; without it the pool's first close would take the
    database with it.
-->
<Configure id="sample" class="org.eclipse.jetty.ee11.webapp.WebAppContext">

    <New id="xldrDataSource" class="org.eclipse.jetty.plus.jndi.Resource">
        <Arg><Ref refid="sample"/></Arg>
        <Arg>jdbc/xldr</Arg>
        <Arg>
            <New class="org.h2.jdbcx.JdbcDataSource">
                <Set name="URL">
                    jdbc:h2:mem:sample;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1;INIT=CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS customer(id INTEGER, name VARCHAR(50), source VARCHAR(20))
                </Set>
                <Set name="user">sa</Set>
                <Set name="password"></Set>
            </New>
        </Arg>
    </New>

</Configure>
